>
  <p>
    Skin problems in the genital area, groin and other intimate sites are common, and they are
    among the most frequently misdiagnosed conditions in dermatology — largely because people
    delay getting them looked at, and treat them with whatever is available over the counter in
    the meantime.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is ordinary clinical work. Venereology is part of the DDVL qualification, and the
    consultation is conducted the same way as one for a rash on an arm.
  </p>

  <h2>What is commonly seen</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fungal infection of the groin</strong> — very common, often recurrent, and frequently worsened by combination steroid creams</li>
    <li><strong>Eczema and contact dermatitis</strong> — from soaps, wipes, fragrance, latex or detergents</li>
    <li><strong>Lichen sclerosus</strong> — pale, thinned, fragile skin, often itchy. Needs treatment because untreated disease causes scarring</li>
    <li><strong>Lichen planus</strong> — including forms affecting the mouth and genital skin together</li>
    <li><strong>Psoriasis in the folds</strong> — shiny and red rather than scaly, so often not recognised</li>
    <li><strong>Scrotal and vulval dermatitis</strong> — persistent itch and burning, frequently treated for years as a fungal problem</li>
    <li><strong>Warts and molluscum contagiosum</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Normal anatomical variants</strong> — pearly penile papules, Fordyce spots, vestibular papillae. No treatment needed, and reassurance is the treatment</li>
    <li><strong>Sexually transmitted infections</strong> — assessed and tested where relevant</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why these drag on</h2>

  <p>
    Intimate skin is thin, warm and occluded. It reacts differently to the same conditions seen
    elsewhere, so the textbook appearance is often absent. Steroid creams are applied early
    because they relieve itching, which masks fungal infection and changes how everything looks.
    And people wait, sometimes years, before having it examined.
  </p>

  <p>
    The result is a cycle of partial treatment. Breaking it starts with establishing what the
    condition actually is — which in this area frequently means a KOH mount, a swab, or a small
    biopsy rather than another cream.
  </p>

  <h2>What a consultation involves</h2>

  <p>
    A history — how long, what it feels like, what has been applied, what else is going on
    medically. Then examination, and the relevant test. You will be told what is being looked for
    and why.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the condition is one that needs examination or a sample, and you have booked online, that
    will be said at the start rather than after payment.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Bring the creams</h3>
    <p>
      Every tube that has been used on the area, including anything bought without a prescription
      and anything recommended by a chemist. In this part of the body, knowing exactly what was
      applied often changes the diagnosis rather than just the treatment.
    </p>
  </div>
